1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,493
AI is changing the tasks inside entry-level technical jobs,

2
00:00:10,494 --> 00:00:14,936
faster than degree programmes and career advice can follow.

3
00:00:15,986 --> 00:00:21,182
This is SkillTide: a chart of that change, for students about to graduate into it.

4
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:29,001
In Malaysia, 56% of working professionals already use AI tools at work.

5
00:00:29,002 --> 00:00:32,583
Only 26% have been trained to use them.

6
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:39,393
TalentCorp expects about 697,000 workers to be significantly affected by AI,

7
00:00:39,394 --> 00:00:44,862
digitalisation and the green economy within three to five years.

8
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:48,833
For a final-year student, that leaves three gaps:

9
00:00:48,834 --> 00:00:52,528
no live view of the change, no evidence behind the advice,

10
00:00:52,529 --> 00:00:55,080
and no plan that fits final year.

11
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:03,601
SkillTide turns dated public signals into an AI-exposure estimate for every task in a job,

12
00:01:03,602 --> 00:01:07,108
then rolls tasks up into roles and skills.

13
00:01:07,558 --> 00:01:12,231
A student moves through four steps, with a guide that explains every answer.

14
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:20,594
Meet Kavin, a synthetic final-year mechatronics student in Johor.

15
00:01:20,744 --> 00:01:23,047
His CV is read right in the browser.

16
00:01:23,048 --> 00:01:24,777
Nothing is uploaded.

17
00:01:25,127 --> 00:01:30,004
SkillTide finds twelve skills and grades each by where it appears:

18
00:01:30,005 --> 00:01:33,477
PLC, SCADA and maintenance from his internship;

19
00:01:33,478 --> 00:01:36,580
robotics, vision and Python from projects;

20
00:01:36,581 --> 00:01:39,690
control and sensors from coursework.

21
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:46,665
The Skills Radar marks 57 skills and 30 roles as rising, steady,

22
00:01:46,666 --> 00:01:49,302
transforming or under pressure.

23
00:01:49,752 --> 00:01:52,273
Each estimate is built task by task:

24
00:01:52,274 --> 00:01:58,256
how people already use AI on it, how far AI could go, and how hiring is moving.

25
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,110
Matches rank 30 roles with a visible formula:

26
00:02:04,111 --> 00:02:09,487
skills fit, AI outlook and SDG fit, weighted the way Kavin chooses.

27
00:02:09,737 --> 00:02:13,271
With no goals set, Mechatronics Engineer comes first.

28
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:19,590
Kavin cares about industry and innovation, and about less waste,

29
00:02:19,591 --> 00:02:22,291
so he picks SDG 9 and SDG 12.

30
00:02:24,041 --> 00:02:27,168
Manufacturing Engineer now leads with 56,

31
00:02:27,169 --> 00:02:31,279
and each SDG link names the real task behind it.

32
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:36,982
Every signal is dated, so the forecast can be replayed.

33
00:02:37,532 --> 00:02:39,038
With March 2025 data,

34
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:44,273
the same profile pointed to Electro-Mechanical & Mechatronics Technician.

35
00:02:44,274 --> 00:02:48,094
With today's data, it's Manufacturing Engineer.

36
00:02:49,044 --> 00:02:52,642
The Guide notices, re-plans, and says what replaced what.

37
00:02:52,643 --> 00:02:56,753
Replays use today's catalogue, so they are not a backtest.

38
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:05,360
Nobody knows how fast AI will spread, so SkillTide runs two scenarios side by side,

39
00:03:05,361 --> 00:03:08,424
changing only the adoption assumption.

40
00:03:09,074 --> 00:03:12,061
Manufacturing Engineer leads in both,

41
00:03:12,062 --> 00:03:17,032
but under fast adoption its outlook drops from 73 to 67.

42
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:21,336
Kavin leaves with a plan:

43
00:03:21,337 --> 00:03:24,955
45 hours over six weeks, at eight hours a week.

44
00:03:25,205 --> 00:03:31,415
Two free courses, then a 20-hour portfolio project built on a real task of the role.

45
00:03:31,765 --> 00:03:35,138
Every course links to where its hours and cost were read,

46
00:03:35,139 --> 00:03:38,843
and a gap with no verified course is named, not filled.

47
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,567
Career centres run the same engine across a synthetic cohort,

48
00:03:44,568 --> 00:03:47,722
to see which rising skills few students have.

49
00:03:48,272 --> 00:03:50,885
Scoring never uses names or backgrounds,

50
00:03:50,886 --> 00:03:54,477
and every ranking is re-run for eight profile variants.

51
00:03:54,478 --> 00:03:56,429
It must stay identical.

52
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:03,986
Under the hood, one Python script rebuilds the dataset from 13 public sources.

53
00:04:04,336 --> 00:04:08,105
Every Monday, GitHub Actions refreshes it, runs the tests,

54
00:04:08,106 --> 00:04:10,634
and redeploys only if they pass.

55
00:04:10,984 --> 00:04:15,158
No language model runs in SkillTide, so it cannot invent a job,

56
00:04:15,159 --> 00:04:16,999
a course or a number.

57
00:04:17,449 --> 00:04:21,424
Twelve synthetic test cases check coverage, budgets and fairness.

58
00:04:21,425 --> 00:04:24,932
They are design checks, not proof of job outcomes.

59
00:04:27,150 --> 00:04:29,309
Each layer reads only the one above it,

60
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:32,526
so every new source flows through to every screen.

61
00:04:32,976 --> 00:04:34,784
Next: more Malaysian signals,

62
00:04:34,785 --> 00:04:39,225
from the critical occupations list and national job-market data.

63
00:04:39,675 --> 00:04:43,867
Testing with final-year volunteers, Bahasa Malaysia and Chinese,

64
00:04:43,868 --> 00:04:45,955
and a low-bandwidth mode.

65
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:51,860
SkillTide. Chart your course before you graduate.
